{"author":"lars@redhat.com","body":"A chart that is not wanted on a cluster still gets an ArgoCD Application. The\nchart's `enabled: false` makes it render nothing, so the Application syncs\ncleanly, reports Healthy, and manages zero resources.\n\nThat is the problem. It introduces a third state — present but inert — where\nthere used to be two. The presence of an Application on a cluster used to tell\nyou the component was live there. It no longer does, and nothing about the\nApplication's name, status or health distinguishes a working component from a\nswitched-off one. You have to go read a values file to find out.\n\nThe guards exist because of how the fan-out works. Every managed ApplicationSet\nis a matrix of `clusterDecisionResource × a constant list of components`. A\ncluster that matches the `gpu-clusters` placement gets an Application for all\nnine components in that list, whether or not it wants them, and the guard\ninside the chart is the only way to say no.\n\nThe coupling that keeps generating guards is the RHOAI major version. RHOAI 2\nand RHOAI 3 want different companion operators, and several of those operators\nconflict with their opposite number. The version is currently stated in\n`values/<hub>/<cluster>/rhoai.yaml`, and the consequences of it are re-asserted\nby hand in three or four sibling files, each with a comment explaining the link.\n\n\n- An Application exists on a cluster if and only if that component is active\n  there. No present-but-inert Applications.\n- Remove the whole-chart `enabled` guard from `rhoai`, `jobset-operator`,\n  `kueue-operator` and `servicemesh-operator`.\n- A cluster's RHOAI major is stated exactly once, in one place.\n- Mutually exclusive choices are unrepresentable rather than merely\n  discouraged. It must not be possible to express \"RHOAI 2 and 3 on the same\n  cluster\", or \"Service Mesh 2 and 3 on the same cluster\", by editing one file\n  and forgetting another.\n- OpenShift Serverless is actually uninstalled from `oac-dev-workload0`, not\n  merely unmanaged. It is installed there today by mistake (§7).\n\n\n- The thirteen other charts with an `enabled:` key. Those are sub-feature\n  toggles inside a chart that is genuinely wanted (`cert-manager`'s\n  ClusterIssuers, `hosted-cluster`'s optional pieces, and so on). They do not\n  create inert Applications and are out of scope.\n- Changing RHOAI component configuration, subscription channels, hardware\n  profiles, or any rendered resource. This change is about which Applications\n  exist, not what they deploy.\n- Changing how ACM placements and the `clusterDecisionResource` generator reach\n  ArgoCD.\n- Migrating `oac-prod-workload0` from RHOAI 2 to RHOAI 3. It stays on 2.\n\n\nThese would count as failure even if the result worked:\n\n- Replacing one boolean with two independent booleans — two feature labels, two\n  placements keyed on separate keys, or two entries in a list — such that\n  setting both is representable and only review catches it.\n- Relocating the guard rather than deleting it. A Helm `condition:` on a\n  dependency, or a values key the ApplicationSet sets, is the same inert state\n  one level down.\n- Any design where the RHOAI major is asserted in two files that can disagree.\n- Requiring per-cluster or per-hub scaffolding files for clusters that do not\n  run RHOAI at all.\n\n\n- All Applications are `prune: true`, `selfHeal: true`, and managed. Sync policy\n  cannot be temporarily relaxed on an individual Application. Migration has to\n  be prune-safe by construction.\n- `charts/*/charts/` and `charts/*/Chart.lock` are gitignored. ArgoCD resolves\n  `file://../<chart>` dependencies at render time, so adding a Helm dependency\n  costs no vendored artifact and no commit churn.\n- `charts/acm-placements` renders only `matchLabels`, plus a hardcoded\n  `local-cluster DoesNotExist` predicate. A new placement is a values entry, not\n  a template change.\n- Hosted-cluster labels are applied to the ManagedCluster by a patch-operator\n  `Patch` (`charts/hosted-cluster/templates/clusterlabels.yaml`), asynchronously\n  after ACM imports the cluster. The patch is a JSON merge patch, so it adds and\n  updates the listed keys and never removes a key that is dropped from values.\n- `helm-unittest` renders the whole chart regardless of the `templates:` filter,\n  so assertions scope but do not limit rendering.\n- RHOAI 3 cannot be reached by upgrading an installed RHOAI 2.\n\n\nClusters carrying `massopen.cloud/feature-gpu: \"true\"`, and therefore matched by\nthe `gpu-clusters` placement:\n\n| Cluster | RHOAI | Mesh | jobset | kueue |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `oac-dev-workload0` | 3 | 3 | on | on |\n| `oac-prod-workload0` | 2 (default) | 2 (default) | off | off |\n\n`gpu-clusters.yaml` fans out nine components to both. On\n`oac-prod-workload0`, `jobset-operator` and `kueue-operator` are the inert\nApplications that prompted this work.\n\nThere is also a live bug. `serverless-operator` has no guard at all, so\n`oac-dev-workload0` installs OpenShift Serverless today even though it runs\nRHOAI 3, which dropped KServe Serverless entirely. Removing it is in scope; see\n§7.\n\nArgoCD on these hubs tracks resources by **annotation**, not by label —\n`argocd-cm` has `application.resourceTrackingMethod: annotation`, set by the\nOpenShift GitOps operator's default rather than by\n`charts/openshift-gitops`. Live resources carry\n`argocd.argoproj.io/tracking-id: <app>:<group>/<kind>:<ns>/<name>`. This matters\nfor the Application renames in §9.\n\n\n\nA new label on the ManagedCluster:\n\n```\nmassopen.cloud/rhoai-version: v2 | v3\n```\n\nAbsent means the cluster runs no RHOAI. This is the whole mechanism that makes\nthe rest safe: **a label key holds exactly one value**, so a cluster cannot be\nin both cohorts. The exclusivity is structural, not conventional.\n\nThe values are `v2` and `v3`, not `2` and `3`, so that they cannot be written\nas YAML integers. A label value must be a string; `massopen.cloud/rhoai-version: 2`\nwith the quotes forgotten is an invalid manifest, and the quoting is easy to\ndrop when editing `clusterLabels` by hand. The prefixed form removes the trap\nand matches the `rhoai-v2` / `rhoai-v3` chart and placement names.\n\nThe label is set in `hosted-clusters/<hub>/<cluster>/values.yaml` under\n`clusterLabels`, which is already in git and already rendered to a patch-operator\n`Patch`. Set `v3` on `oac-dev-workload0` and `v2` on `oac-prod-workload0`.\n\n`massopen.cloud/feature-gpu` keeps its existing meaning — this cluster has GPU\nhardware — and keeps driving `nfd-operator` and the three `nvidia-*` charts.\nRHOAI major is a separate axis and gets its own key.\n\nThe label is the single source of truth. `rhoaiVersion` and `meshVersion`\ndisappear from per-cluster values files entirely — the first because which\nchart is deployed implies the version (§4), the second because the appset\nsupplies it (§6).\n\n\nAdded to `values/<hub>/local-cluster/acm-placements.yaml`:\n\n```yaml\n  - name: rhoai-v2-clusters\n    matchLabels:\n      massopen.cloud/rhoai-version: v2\n  - name: rhoai-v3-clusters\n    matchLabels:\n      massopen.cloud/rhoai-version: v3\n```\n\n**On all three hubs, including `nist-dev-infra`, which runs no RHOAI.**\n*(Builder's call.)* `applicationsets/` renders every template under\n`templates/managed/` on every hub — `hubName` is the only per-hub input — so\n`nist-dev-infra` gets both ApplicationSets whether or not the placements exist.\n\nA placement matching zero clusters is verified harmless. On the dev hub,\n`github-oauth-clusters` selects 0 clusters, ACM still creates\n`github-oauth-clusters-decision-1`, and the ApplicationSet reports\n`ErrorOccurred: False` / \"All applications have been generated successfully\"\nwhile generating zero Applications.\n\nWhat happens with a placement that is *absent* rather than empty is not\nverified. The `clusterDecisionResource` generator lists `PlacementDecision`\nresources by label selector, and an empty list most likely yields an empty\nparameter set on the same no-error path — but confirming that would mean\ncreating a throwaway ApplicationSet on a live hub, and nothing requires us to\nfind out. Defining the placements everywhere sidesteps the question.\n\nIt is also what the repo already does: all three hubs carry an identical set of\nseven placements today, including `gpu-clusters` and `hypervisor-clusters` on\n`nist-dev-infra`, which has no hosted clusters at all.\n\nThe alternative — gating ApplicationSets per hub — would be new machinery that\nnothing else in the repo needs, and runs into the anti-goal about per-hub\nscaffolding.\n\n\nNew `applicationsets/templates/managed/rhoai-v2-clusters.yaml` and\n`rhoai-v3-clusters.yaml`, modelled on `gpu-clusters.yaml`.\n\nv2 component list:\n\n- `rhoai-v2`\n- `servicemesh-operator` (with `meshVersion=2`, see §6)\n- `serverless-operator`\n\nv3 component list:\n\n- `rhoai-v3`\n- `servicemesh-operator` (with `meshVersion=3`)\n\n`jobset-operator` and `kueue-operator` do not appear in either list; they arrive\nas Helm dependencies of `rhoai-v3` (§4).\n\n`gpu-clusters.yaml` is reduced to the components that are genuinely about GPU\nhardware:\n\n- `nfd-operator`\n- `nvidia-gpu-operator`\n- `nvidia-maintenance-operator`\n- `nvidia-network-operator`\n- the commented-out `sriov-network-operator` line stays as-is\n\n\nThe existing chart already keeps the two majors apart —\n`templates/v2/_datasciencecluster.tpl` and `templates/v3/_datasciencecluster.tpl`,\na `dataScienceCluster.v2` / `.v3` values split, a `channels` map keyed by\nversion, and an `ne .rhoaiVersion \"3\"` guard on `odhdashboardconfig.yaml`. The\nsplit turns those branches into two charts, each with no branching.\n\n`charts/rhoai-v2`:\n\n- `operator.yaml` with `subscription.channel: stable` hardcoded\n- `datasciencecluster.yaml` rendering the v1 DataScienceCluster body directly\n- `odhdashboardconfig.yaml` unchanged, unguarded\n- `dataScienceCluster` values un-nested from under `v2:`\n- `Chart.yaml` dependencies: `operator-library`, plus `hardware-profiles` with\n  `apiVersion: infrastructure.opendatahub.io/v1alpha1`\n\n`charts/rhoai-v3`:\n\n- `operator.yaml` with `subscription.channel: stable-3.5` hardcoded\n- `datasciencecluster.yaml` rendering the v2 DataScienceCluster body directly\n- no `odhdashboardconfig.yaml`\n- `dataScienceCluster` values un-nested from under `v3:`\n- `Chart.yaml` dependencies: `operator-library`, plus `hardware-profiles` with\n  `apiVersion: infrastructure.opendatahub.io/v1`, plus **unconditional**\n  `jobset-operator` and `kueue-operator` via `file://../`\n\nThe channels are hardcoded rather than kept as single-entry `channels` maps.\nThe per-cluster `subscription.channel` override that motivated the map — how a\ncluster takes `eus-3.5` instead of `stable-3.5` — still works.\n\n\nThe HardwareProfile catalogue is shared rather than duplicated: a new\n`hardware-profiles` chart holding the `hardwareProfiles` catalogue in its own\n`values.yaml`, the `enabledHardwareProfiles` filtering, and the\nHardwareProfile template. Both RHOAI charts take it as a `file://../`\ndependency and set `apiVersion` to their own, which is the only thing that\ndiffers between the two renderings.\n\nA dependency rather than a library chart, so the catalogue stays values data —\neditable, and overridable per hub or per cluster — instead of becoming a\nliteral inside a `_helpers.tpl`. It also gets its own helm-unittest suite,\nwhich the current `hardwareprofile_test.yaml` becomes.\n\nThe cost is Helm's subchart values nesting: per-cluster selection moves from a\nflat `enabledHardwareProfiles` to\n\n```yaml\nhardware-profiles:\n  enabledHardwareProfiles:\n    - nvidia-h100-gpu\n```\n\nin `values/<hub>/<cluster>/rhoai-v2.yaml` and `rhoai-v3.yaml`. Two files, and\n§8 covers them.\n\nThe dependencies are unconditional on purpose. A `condition:` key can only read\na values path — Helm cannot compute it from the version — so a conditional\ndependency would be the guard again. In a chart that only exists for v3, an\nunconditional dependency carries no state.\n\n`charts/rhoai` is deleted. `enabledHardwareProfiles` and the `hardwareProfiles`\ncatalogue are duplicated into both charts, or factored into a shared library\nchart; see \"Left to the builder\".\n\n\nBoth keep their charts and their tests. Both lose the top-level `enabled` key\nand the `{{- if .Values.enabled }}` wrapper in every template. Neither appears\nin any ApplicationSet component list any more.\n\n`kueue-operator` **must** leave `gpu-clusters`. If it stayed there while also\nbeing a dependency of `rhoai-v3`, two Applications would manage the same\n`Kueue`, `ClusterQueue` and `LocalQueue` resources on a v3 cluster and fight.\n\nToday no non-RHOAI cluster wants Kueue standalone. If one ever does, it gets\npromoted back to a component in its own right — and `rhoai-v3` must then drop\nthe dependency, not both.\n\nSync ordering is already handled for the dry run: `JobSetOperator` and `Kueue`\nboth carry `SkipDryRunOnMissingResource=true`, and `operator-library` places the\nnamespace, OperatorGroup and Subscription at sync-wave 0.\n\nWhat *does* change is cold-install behaviour. Today the three operators are\nthree Applications that fail independently. Folded together, all three\nSubscriptions land at wave 0 and `DataScienceCluster`, `Kueue` and\n`JobSetOperator` all land at wave 1 — and wave 1 fires when wave 0 is applied,\nnot when three separate OLM installs have finished producing their CRDs.\n`SkipDryRunOnMissingResource` gets past the dry run, but the real apply still\nfails if the CRD is not there yet, and one failing CR now fails the whole RHOAI\nsync with `retry.limit: 5` backing off to 10m. Irrelevant to this migration,\nwhere everything already exists, but it bites the next clean RHOAI 3 build.\n\n**`rhoai-v3` pushes the subchart CRs to a later sync wave than its own**\n*(builder's call)* — the `JobSetOperator` and `Kueue` CRs move to wave 2 or\nbeyond via a value the parent sets, so a cold install converges on the first\nsync instead of self-healing through a retry loop. It is cheap now and awkward\nto retrofit once the charts and their tests exist.\n\n\nThe chart is a single Subscription whose only version-dependent field is the\npackage name (`servicemeshoperator` vs `servicemeshoperator3`). Splitting it\ninto two charts to vary one string is worse than keeping the lookup.\n\nInstead:\n\n- Delete the `enabled` guard.\n- `meshVersion` stops being a per-cluster value. Each RHOAI ApplicationSet\n  supplies it as a `helm.parameters` entry: `meshVersion=2` in the v2 appset,\n  `meshVersion=3` in the v3 appset.\n- Delete `values/oac-dev-infra/oac-dev-workload0/servicemesh-operator.yaml`.\n\nThis is still one source of truth. Which appset a cluster is in is determined by\nthe label, so the parameter cannot disagree with it.\n\nThe `names` map and the `subscription.name` override stay, so a cluster can\nstill pin an explicit package name if it ever needs to.\n\n\nMoving the cluster into the v3 cohort removes `serverless-operator` from its\ncomponent list, which deletes the Application. That is necessary but not\nsufficient, and it collides with the migration's safety flag: step 1 of §9 sets\n`preserveResourcesOnDeletion: true` on `gpu-clusters` precisely so that removing\ncomponents does *not* delete their resources. Serverless would therefore be\norphaned on the cluster — still installed, no longer managed — which is a worse\nstate than today's.\n\nSo the uninstall is an explicit, separate action, not a consequence.\n\nLive state as of 2026-10-21, checked against the cluster:\n\n- `serverless-operator.v1.37.2` is installed from a Subscription in\n  `openshift-serverless`, with an AllNamespaces OperatorGroup, so the CSV is\n  copied into every namespace.\n- There are **no** `KnativeServing`, `KnativeEventing`, `Service` (ksvc) or\n  `Revision` resources anywhere on the cluster. Nothing is using it.\n- The `knative-serving`, `knative-serving-ingress` and `knative-eventing`\n  namespaces exist, are 40 days old, and are empty of Knative CRs. They are\n  leftovers; ArgoCD does not track them (no `tracking-id` annotation).\n- Only the `openshift-serverless` namespace carries a tracking-id, from\n  `oac-dev-workload0-serverless-operator`.\n\nBecause nothing consumes it, removal is clean. After the Application is gone,\ndelete by hand on `oac-dev-workload0`: the Subscription and CSV in\n`openshift-serverless`, then the `openshift-serverless`, `knative-serving`,\n`knative-serving-ingress` and `knative-eventing` namespaces, then any\n`*.knative.dev` CRDs the operator left behind. Re-check for Knative CRs\nimmediately before deleting, in case something has been created since.\n\n`oac-prod-workload0` stays on RHOAI 2 and keeps Serverless via the v2 appset.\nNothing changes there.\n\n\nComponent values files are named after the component, so renaming the component\nrenames the file:\n\n- `values/oac-dev-infra/oac-dev-workload0/rhoai.yaml` → `rhoai-v3.yaml`, with\n  `rhoaiVersion: 3` removed and `enabledHardwareProfiles` re-nested under\n  `hardware-profiles:` (the comment about this cluster's H100s stays)\n- `values/oac-prod-infra/oac-prod-workload0/rhoai.yaml` → `rhoai-v2.yaml`, with\n  the same re-nesting (the comment about both H100s and A100s stays)\n- `values/oac-dev-infra/oac-dev-workload0/jobset-operator.yaml` — deleted, it\n  contains only `enabled: true`\n- `values/oac-dev-infra/oac-dev-workload0/kueue-operator.yaml` — deleted, same\n- `values/oac-dev-infra/oac-dev-workload0/servicemesh-operator.yaml` — deleted\n\nThe re-nesting is the one behaviour-affecting edit in this step, and it is the\nexact hazard §11 calls out: `enabledHardwareProfiles` left at the top level\nwould render as no selected profiles rather than failing, silently pruning the\nHardwareProfiles from both clusters.\n\nIf a cluster ever needs to override another subchart's values, those go under a\n`jobset-operator:` or `kueue-operator:` key inside `rhoai-v3.yaml`, per the same\nHelm nesting rule.\n\n\nThis is the risky part and the order matters.\n\n**`gpu-clusters` does not set `preserveResourcesOnDeletion`.** Unlike\n`all-managed-clusters`, which sets it `true`, `gpu-clusters` leaves it at the\ndefault of `false`. Removing a component from its list therefore deletes the\nApplication *and everything it manages*. Done naively, this change would delete\nthe RHOAI operator and the DataScienceCluster from both GPU clusters.\n\n**The label is the only thing that can stage this per hub.** `bootstrap`\nrenders `applicationsets/` from `main` on every hub, and the component lists\nlive in the templates, so no commit can reach one hub and not the other. Dev\ngoes first by being labelled first, not by landing different code.\n\n**Deliberately, no cluster is labelled until the new machinery is already in\nplace and inert.** That is what removes the Application name-collision hazard\nrather than just narrowing it: three of the moved components keep their names\nacross the move (`oac-prod-workload0-servicemesh-operator`,\n`oac-dev-workload0-servicemesh-operator`,\n`oac-prod-workload0-serverless-operator`), because both the old and new\nApplicationSets use the same `{{name}}-{{component}}` template. If the cohort\nappsets generated anything while `gpu-clusters` still listed those components,\ntwo ApplicationSet controllers would briefly own one Application name — a\nrefusal to adopt, or an ownerReference flip-flop that deletes and recreates a\nlive Application. With no labels set, the cohort appsets generate zero\nApplications, so the collision never exists.\n\nOne commit per step.\n\n1. Add `syncPolicy.preserveResourcesOnDeletion: true` to `gpu-clusters.yaml`.\n   Confirm it is live on both hubs before going further.\n2. Add the two placements to all three hubs. Confirm the `Placement` resources\n   exist and resolve to zero clusters — no cluster carries the label yet.\n3. Add the two new charts and the two new ApplicationSets. Confirm both\n   ApplicationSets exist on both hubs and generate **zero** Applications.\n4. Remove the five moved components from `gpu-clusters`. The old Applications\n   are deleted with their resources preserved. Confirm on both clusters that\n   the RHOAI operator, `default-dsc`, the mesh Subscription and (on prod)\n   Serverless are all still running and untouched.\n5. Rename the per-cluster values files and delete the three guard-only ones\n   (§8). Nothing reads them at this point, so this is inert.\n6. Label `oac-dev-workload0` `massopen.cloud/rhoai-version: v3`. Confirm the\n   patch-operator `Patch` lands it on the ManagedCluster — this is\n   asynchronous — then that `rhoai-v3-clusters` generates\n   `oac-dev-workload0-rhoai-v3` and `-servicemesh-operator`, that both adopt\n   the preserved resources, and that both reach Synced/Healthy. Soak.\n7. Uninstall the orphaned Serverless on `oac-dev-workload0` (§7). After the\n   Application is gone, so ArgoCD cannot re-create what you delete.\n8. Label `oac-prod-workload0` `massopen.cloud/rhoai-version: v2`. Same\n   verification: `rhoai-v2-clusters` generates `-rhoai-v2`,\n   `-servicemesh-operator` and `-serverless-operator`, all adopting cleanly.\n9. Decide whether to revert `preserveResourcesOnDeletion` on `gpu-clusters`.\n\n**The cost of going dev-first is an unmanaged window on prod**, from step 4\nuntil step 8. RHOAI 2, Service Mesh 2 and Serverless keep running on\n`oac-prod-workload0` with their resources intact, but no Application owns them,\nso `selfHeal` is not correcting drift and a chart change pushed during the\nwindow will not be applied. Nothing is deleted. The window can be closed as soon\nas dev's cutover is verified rather than after a full soak — step 8 does not\ndepend on step 7.\n\nTwo things to verify during steps 6 and 8. These hubs track resources by annotation —\n`argocd-cm` carries `application.resourceTrackingMethod: annotation` — so every\nmanaged resource holds an `argocd.argoproj.io/tracking-id` naming the\nApplication that owns it. Renaming an Application (`<cluster>-rhoai` →\n`<cluster>-rhoai-v3`) leaves the existing resources holding the old name, so the\nnew Application sees them as owned by another app. Expect it to apply over them\nand rewrite the annotation, possibly after a \"resource is already managed by\nanother application\" warning. Confirm this resolves rather than wedging.\nSecond, the new charts must render identical resource names, namespaces and\nkinds to what the old `rhoai` chart produced for that cluster, or adoption turns\ninto a delete-and-recreate of the DataScienceCluster.\n\n\nFlipping a cluster's `massopen.cloud/rhoai-version` from `v2` to `v3` drops it\nout of one placement and into the other. The v2 Application is deleted and a v3\nApplication is created in its place. Whether the deletion takes the RHOAI 2\ninstall with it depends on `preserveResourcesOnDeletion` on the new\nApplicationSets.\n\n**Decision: `preserveResourcesOnDeletion: true` on both new ApplicationSets, and\na `v2`→`v3` flip is not a supported operation.** A bare label edit therefore orphans\nRHOAI 2 — a visible wrong state — rather than triggering an async teardown\nArgoCD will not wait for. The design says so explicitly; the uninstall procedure\ngets written when prod actually moves, not now.\n\n**This is a prod-only concern.** `oac-dev-workload0` is already on 3 and\n`oac-prod-workload0` stays on 2, so nothing in this migration performs a flip.\nIt matters for whenever prod moves.\n\nLive state checked 2026-10-21, because the risk is often described wrongly:\n\n- `DataScienceCluster/default-dsc` has **no finalizers** on either cluster —\n  `datasciencecluster.opendatahub.io/v1` under rhods-operator 2.25.11 on prod,\n  `/v2` under 3.5.2 on dev. `OdhDashboardConfig` has none either.\n- `DSCInitialization/default-dsci` carries\n  `dscinitialization.opendatahub.io/finalizer` on both.\n- `KnativeServing/knative-serving` is live and Ready on `oac-prod-workload0`,\n  owned by RHOAI 2's kserve Serverless mode.\n\nSo pruning is not blocked by finalizers on the DSC. The risks are:\n\n1. **The delete returns before the teardown happens.** With no finalizer on the\n   DSC, the API accepts the delete immediately, ArgoCD marks it pruned, and the\n   operator is still asynchronously unwinding dashboard, workbenches, kserve,\n   ray, kueue, trustyai and pipelines. The Application reports done while the\n   cluster is not, and nothing in its health surfaces the gap.\n2. **Prune order is correct but not synchronous.** Argo prunes in reverse\n   sync-wave order, so the Subscription (wave 0) goes after the DSC (wave 1).\n   But \"after\" means immediately after, not after the operator finished, so the\n   controller can vanish mid-teardown and leave components half-removed with\n   nothing left to finish them.\n3. **Live Knative on prod.** If the operator goes before kserve's teardown\n   completes, `oac-prod-workload0` inherits exactly the orphaned-Serverless\n   state §7 exists to clean off dev.\n4. **Re-flipping does not undo it.** Relabelling back to `v2` reinstalls the\n   operator and a fresh DSC, but user workbench PVCs and anything the components\n   owned in project namespaces are gone.\n\n**The `DSCInitialization` gap is unhandled either way.** The `rhoai` chart has no\nDSCI template — the operator creates the object itself — so it is the one\nfinalizer-bearing resource in the stack that no Application manages. Pruning the\nApplication removes the Subscription and CSV and leaves the DSCI behind with no\noperator left to clear its finalizer; anything that later deletes the DSCI or\nthe `redhat-ods-operator` namespace hangs, which is the shape of the\nhosted-cluster deprovision deadlock. Preserving resources leaves it behind too,\njust alongside everything else. Whichever option is chosen, a RHOAI 2 uninstall\nprocedure has to deal with the DSCI explicitly, and the split charts should not\npretend to own it.\n\n\n- `charts/rhoai/tests/unit/enabled_test.yaml` and `version_test.yaml` go away\n  with the chart; their intent splits across the two new charts' tests.\n- The existing `datasciencecluster_test.yaml` and `datasciencecluster-v3_test.yaml`\n  become the v2 and v3 charts' tests respectively, with the version-selection\n  assertions dropped.\n- New fail-closed tests for the two new ApplicationSets, matching the pattern in\n  `applicationsets/tests/unit/gpu-clusters_test.yaml`, including a test that\n  `gpu-clusters` no longer lists the five moved components.\n- A test that `rhoai-v3` renders the JobSet and Kueue resources from its\n  dependencies, which is what makes the dependency wiring observable.\n- `scripts/helm-lint-all.sh` and `scripts/helm-unittest-all.sh` must pass.\n  `helm-update-deps.sh` needs to pick up the new `file://` dependencies.\n- `charts/hardware-profiles` takes over the existing `hardwareprofile_test.yaml`,\n  extended to cover both apiVersions and the `enabledHardwareProfiles` filter,\n  including the empty-selection case.\n- Guard the empty-render hazard with **fail-closed helm-unittest assertions, not\n  a script**. Un-nesting `dataScienceCluster.v2`/`.v3`, and re-nesting\n  `enabledHardwareProfiles` under `hardware-profiles:`, are exactly the kind of\n  one-level values-key moves that Helm renders as an empty string rather than\n  failing. Each of the two new charts needs assertions that pin the concrete\n  value of every field moving out from under those keys — every component\n  managementState, the kserve block, `registriesNamespace` — such that a\n  silently-empty render fails the test rather than passing with `\"\"`. The\n  HardwareProfile case fails differently and needs its own assertion: a\n  mis-nested `enabledHardwareProfiles` renders *zero* profiles rather than an\n  empty string, so assert the expected profile names are present for each\n  cluster's values.\n  `scripts/check_empty_attrs.py` is referenced for this elsewhere but is not in\n  the repo (only a stale `__pycache__/check_empty_attrs.cpython-314.pyc`; the\n  `.py` was never committed), and writing it is deliberately out of scope —\n  targeted assertions on the fields that actually move are narrower and aimed at\n  this change. `scripts/uninstall-rhoai-2x.sh`, referenced by\n  `charts/rhoai/values.yaml`, is missing in the same way; also out of scope,\n  per §10.\n\n\nA series of commits on a branch, **one per numbered step in §9**, so the\nsequence is legible in the history and each step can be rolled out and verified\non its own. Lars manages pull requests and drives the rollout; no step is\nmerged or applied to a cluster by the builder.\n\nSteps 6, 7 and 8 are cluster actions rather than code — the label edits are\ncommits, but the verification and the Serverless uninstall are not. Those\ncommits should carry enough detail in their messages to be followed as a\nrunbook.\n\n\n- Test file naming and how finely to split the existing DataScienceCluster\n  snapshot tests.\n- Whether step 8 of the migration reverts `preserveResourcesOnDeletion` on\n  `gpu-clusters`. Leaving it set matches `all-managed-clusters` and is the safer\n  default. Note this is a different question from the same setting on the two\n  *new* ApplicationSets, which §10 covers and which is not the builder's call."}